{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-127238",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "To find the second-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=3,$ we plug $a=3$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(3)+{f}^{′}(3)(x-3)+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(3)(x-3)^{2}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $f(3)=6,$ ${f}^{′}(3)=-5,$ and ${f}^{″}(3)=-6$ into our second-degree Taylor polynomial gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =6-5(x-3)+\\frac{1}{2}(-6)(x-3)^{2} \\\\  & =6-5(x-3)-3(x-3)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
